{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct/near-direct matches to the target concept (nodular goiter) via either nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) or benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional scores (PGS005273–PGS005274) are explicitly case–control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter, which are not pure nodular-goiter risk endpoints and therefore are down-ranked for endpoint fidelity.

Among the pure nodular-goiter endpoints, PGS005262 is preferred because it comes from a disease-focused, multi-cohort/biobank thyroid disease genetics study context (many cohorts listed) and reports the highest visible discrimination metric in this set (full-model AUROC 0.5933). It also uses a genome-wide shrinkage approach (PRSCS) rather than a sparse P+T construction (PGS005261).

Main limitation: for the PGS005261–PGS005263 family, covariates are ‘Unknown’ and the reported AUROC appears to be full-model (not explicitly PRS-only), reducing comparability and making it unclear how much is attributable to the PRS. The UKB portability-framework models (PGS001814/PGS002022) report only small partial correlations (0.0277 and 0.024) despite clearer covariates, creating mixed evidence rather than a single unambiguous best choice; hence confidence is Moderate and outcome is DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target disease concept (nodular goiter) via closely aligned endpoints: two UKB portability-framework scores for "Nontoxic multinodular goiter" (PGS001814, PGS002022) and three disease-focused multi-biobank scores for "Benign nodular goiter" (PGS005261, PGS005262, PGS005263). Two additional candidates (PGS005273, PGS005274) are case-case endpoints (thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter) and are down-ranked for not representing generic nodular goiter risk.

Among the true disease-endpoint models, PGS005262 is preferred because it has the closest phenotype wording to the target (benign nodular goiter), comes from a disease-focused multi-ancestry biobank study (rather than a pan-trait portability paper), and has the strongest visible discrimination in its family (full-model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5854 and 0.5876) alongside a stronger reported per-SD effect size (OR 1.41). 

However, the overall recommendation remains sub-optimal because the AUROCs reported for the benign nodular goiter models appear to be full-model (covariates are "Unknown"), limiting PRS-only comparability, while the UKB portability models provide only weak PRS-comparable metrics (partial-r ~0.02–0.03). This leaves uncertainty about which model has the best PRS-only performance under comparable covariate adjustment."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002033","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 10 candidates are direct concept matches to obesity by trait_efo, but they differ materially in endpoint fidelity and performance evidence. Several have limited or non-comparable metrics (many report only an OR/beta, or only a generic R² without clear PRS-only isolation), and some are framework/portability or integration-context scores with sparse metadata.

PGS002033 is preferred because it has good phenotype alignment for a generic obesity target (trait_reported and phenotyping_reported both reflect overweight/obesity), comes with an explicit PRS-comparable performance signal (partial correlation partial-r=0.0789, with CI provided) in a European validation context, and is based on a genome-wide shrinkage method (LDpred2) rather than very sparse GWS-only constructions. It also has a clear UKB training sample size (n=391,124) and defined basic covariates.

Main limitation: the best-supported alternative set is still competitive and evidence is incomplete—most candidates lack PRS-only AUROC/R², and PGS002033 itself does not report AUROC/R² here (only partial-r), while some obesity-diagnosis-anchored endpoints (e.g., E66/phecode) provide closer clinical endpoint fidelity but do not provide comparable discrimination metrics in the visible context. This prevents a high-confidence, clearly dominant selection."}
